# Rank 1659 / CVE-2025-43865 (GHSA-cpj6-fhp6-mr6j, CWE-345): React Router's
# server runtime trusted the X-React-Router-Prerender-Data request header as a
# build-time signal. Any client sending it on a .data request made the
# data-route loader wrapper skip the real loader and return the header's decoded
# payload as that route's loader data, so the caller chose what the victim's
# page renders. The wrapper in packages/react-router/lib/server-runtime/routes.ts
# did `if (args.request.headers.has("X-React-Router-Prerender-Data"))` and
# returned decodeViaTurboStream's decode of `args.request.headers.get(...)` --
# a header the vite plugin sets only on its own build-time prerender requests,
# which the deployed handler never distinguished from a client request. The
# integration test the fix adds sends the header with
# '[{"_1":2},"routes/_index",{"_3":4},"data","Hello World!"]' and asserts the
# runtime ignores it.
#
# Commit c84302972a ("Adjust approach for prerendering/SPA mode via headers",
# PR #13453) moves the read into getBuildTimeHeader (server-runtime/dev.ts),
# which returns the value only when process.env.IS_RR_BUILD_REQUEST === "yes"
# -- set by the vite plugin around its build-time requests -- and null
# otherwise, so a runtime client's header is ignored and the real loader runs.
# The remediation is present at the fix and the vulnerable read at its parent.
# No pool correction: the row names this repository and this commit, and both
# read as the fixing change.
#
# Held: build-signal header provenance. The defect is which channel a
# privileged decision reads -- a request header any client can send, treated as
# an internal build-time signal with no server-side gate. Nothing can ask "is
# this header read gated by a trusted server-side signal rather than by the
# request itself". Keying on the fix's env var keys on the fixing commit's own
# vocabulary, and the header name is the framework's private channel, so a
# binding naming it detects this framework rather than the weakness. Measured
# on packages/react-router/lib/server-runtime, three --cache off runs per
# revision, deterministic: 22 findings at the parent and 21 at the fix. The one
# row touching the vulnerable return is VYQL-INJ-013, code.DomInput at
# routes.ts:129 flowing to code.ProtoPollute at server.ts:373 -- present at
# both revisions (at the fix the source moves to routes.ts:131 and the sink to
# server.ts:379), so it does not discriminate. The fix adds rows on the new
# helper instead of removing any: ExternalEntryInput twice at dev.ts:19,
# SqlExecution and UrlFetch at dev.ts:23, and two caller-controlled headerName
# rows -- fix-direction noise on a 12-line function. The -flags view drops 9
# flags to 6, but the three that disappear are low-confidence NullableDeref
# flags on routes.ts's function contexts, cleared by the added
# `preRenderedData != null` comparison: a null-check heuristic, not header
# provenance.
#
# The family's ship-blocker lifted after the first pass and still does not
# reach here. core.AuthenticationCheck now also neutralises
# access_control.IncorrectAuthorization, so a sink declaring that threat is no
# longer orphaned in javascript and code.ClientControlledSecurityDecision /
# VYQL-AUTH-098 is shippable in this language. It does not apply: that concept
# reports a client-controlled value driving a role or privilege decision, and
# this defect is a client-controlled value selecting which data serves the
# route. The decision lives in control flow -- whether callRouteHandler runs --
# not on any node the header's taint can reach, so there is no sink to label;
# the only binding today is java's, on bean accessors that read a
# client-chosen authentication mode back.
#
# The expressible approximations were measured with throwaway presence probes
# on the loader wrapper and are co-occurrence, not data flow. Requiring a
# `headers.get` and a decode call in one function labels 3 contexts at the
# parent (the wrapper plus the two functions enclosing it, which inherit nested
# tokens) and 0 at the fix -- but across real trees it reports qwik's
# parseRequest (request-handler/request-event.ts:374), which reads the standard
# content-type header at :379 and decodes a query parameter at :392, two values
# with no relation. Requiring `headers.has` and `headers.get` together reports
# four more on CF-connecting-ip, CF-IPCountry, Cache-Control and Sec-Fetch-Dest.
# react, growi and ToolJet report 0 under every variant. Negating on a
# process.env selector does not remove the qwik row. What separates this defect
# from those reads is which header it is and what the decoded value becomes --
# provenance, not shape -- so no rule keyed on the shape is shippable. No
# ENGINE-GAPS entry: the gap is decision vocabulary, not a localised construct.
# No definition change. Both blocks assert reject VYQL-AUTH-079: when
# build-signal provenance vocabulary lands, the ungated form must flip to
# expect.

test "rank1659: the loader returns the prerender header's payload"
  lang javascript
  file packages/react-router/lib/server-runtime/routes.ts
  reject VYQL-AUTH-079
  code
  ```
  import { decodeViaTurboStream } from "./single-fetch";

  export function createStaticHandlerDataRoutes(routes, callRouteHandler) {
    return routes.map((route) => {
      return {
        id: route.id,
        loader: route.module.loader
          ? async (args) => {
              // If we're prerendering, use the data passed in from
              // prerendering the .data route so we don't call loaders twice
              if (args.request.headers.has("X-React-Router-Prerender-Data")) {
                const preRenderedData = args.request.headers.get(
                  "X-React-Router-Prerender-Data"
                );
                let encoded = preRenderedData
                  ? decodeURI(preRenderedData)
                  : preRenderedData;
                let uint8array = new TextEncoder().encode(encoded);
                let stream = new ReadableStream({
                  start(controller) {
                    controller.enqueue(uint8array);
                    controller.close();
                  },
                });
                let decoded = await decodeViaTurboStream(stream, global);
                let data = decoded.value;
                let result = data[route.id];
                return result.data;
              }
              let val = await callRouteHandler(route.module.loader, args);
              return val;
            }
          : undefined,
      };
    });
  }
  ```

test "rank1659: the fixed revision reads the header only for build-time requests"
  lang javascript
  file packages/react-router/lib/server-runtime/routes.ts
  reject VYQL-AUTH-079
  code
  ```
  import { decodeViaTurboStream } from "./single-fetch";

  // Guarded access to build-time-only headers
  export function getBuildTimeHeader(request, headerName) {
    if (typeof process !== "undefined") {
      try {
        if (process.env?.IS_RR_BUILD_REQUEST === "yes") {
          return request.headers.get(headerName);
        }
      } catch (e) {}
    }
    return null;
  }

  export function createStaticHandlerDataRoutes(routes, callRouteHandler) {
    return routes.map((route) => {
      return {
        id: route.id,
        loader: route.module.loader
          ? async (args) => {
              // If we're prerendering, use the data passed in from
              // prerendering the .data route so we don't call loaders twice
              let preRenderedData = getBuildTimeHeader(
                args.request,
                "X-React-Router-Prerender-Data"
              );
              if (preRenderedData != null) {
                let encoded = preRenderedData
                  ? decodeURI(preRenderedData)
                  : preRenderedData;
                let uint8array = new TextEncoder().encode(encoded);
                let stream = new ReadableStream({
                  start(controller) {
                    controller.enqueue(uint8array);
                    controller.close();
                  },
                });
                let decoded = await decodeViaTurboStream(stream, global);
                let data = decoded.value;
                let result = data[route.id];
                return result.data;
              }
              let val = await callRouteHandler(route.module.loader, args);
              return val;
            }
          : undefined,
      };
    });
  }
  ```
